ement.\n\nThere are two behaviours in play: the \"native\" owner case and the \"external\" owner case. For EACH of the two, determine independently whether the failing/incorrect thing is:\n  - \"obsolete_assertion\"  = the test encodes a superseded contract; the implementation is right\n  - \"implementation_defect\" = the test encodes the current contract correctly; the implementation is wrong\nJudge each case on the evidence from requirements.md vs. the actual code. Do not assume both cases have the same classification.\n\nWHAT TO CHANGE\n1. Fix lifecycle.py and/or test_lifecycle.py so that behaviour matches requirements.md exactly and all tests pass. Only fix what the diagnosis justifies \u2014 do not change a correct behaviour.\n2. Run the tests: python3 -m unittest test_lifecycle -v   (this command is preauthorized). Tests must pass. Paste the real output in your report; never claim a pass you did not observe.\n3. Write diagnosis.json in the project root. It MUST have this exact top-level shape:\n   {\n     \"native\": \"<obsolete_assertion|implementation_defect>\",\n     \"external\": \"<obsolete_assertion|implementation_defect>\",\n     \"evidence\": { ... }\n   }\n   - \"native\" and \"external\" are TOP-LEVEL string values, exactly one of the two allowed strings each.\n   - \"evidence\" is a SEPARATE nested object holding your supporting detail (e.g. per-case: what requirements.md says, what the test asserted, what the code returned, what you changed). Structure inside \"evidence\" is your call, but keep it factual and specific.\n   - Validate it parses: python3 -m json.tool diagnosis.json (preauthorized).\n4. If you notice unrelated improvement ideas (code quality, test structure, repo hygiene), write them to improvements.md in the project root. Do NOT implement them. If you have none, do not create the file.\n\nHARD CONSTRAINTS\n- Implementation changes are limited to lifecycle.py and test_lifecycle.py. New files diagnosis.json and improvements.md are expe